      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's much better, thank you. I'm looking at the transition in your last paragraph where you say \"Therefore, the moral imperative for social democrats is to correct the inherent inequalities...\" It sounds like you're framing the social democratic position as the necessary solution to a problem that libertarianism can't solve. \n\nTo keep my essay balanced, I want to make sure I'm not just presenting one side as the \"fix\" for the other. Could you explore the libertarian critique of that Rawlsian idea? Specifically, how would a libertarian respond to the claim that we have a \"moral imperative\" to correct inequalities? I want to see if they view those inequalities as something that is fundamentally just or if they have a different way of looking at the \"starting line\" problem.",
